#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120707} :activity/id #uuid "28cf8e1a-d07a-556d-a006-2d022a4740c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850 :topic/id #uuid "c60f74c0-bf82-529d-b9dd-b8bfe8e2cc4a" :topic/math-academy-id 1799} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121048 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b2a904a-eda3-58e1-9b0f-e742454f48b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3245 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "762cbf4d-a393-51df-98bb-da96c034848b" :step/math-academy-id 17885 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119637 :question/id #uuid "005f8f3f-89eb-5e99-bf0f-82b67329661a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4910"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387782}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"} :step/id #uuid "0e305d64-a5b0-5e37-85c9-271d9bd1785b" :step/math-academy-id 17886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119638 :question/id #uuid "897aa930-02ca-5f79-95d0-5ee11420da97" :question/math-academy-id "e-4911"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367226}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"} :step/id #uuid "15d988ae-4b19-5d9f-a7e2-fd6fd8c3f4c6" :step/math-academy-id 17887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120728}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119639 :question/id #uuid "df314d1d-56c8-5503-b632-8a425b47070f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4912"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367999}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"} :step/id #uuid "1e9d9f49-2eb3-5745-9da4-70a87dddd9f6" :step/math-academy-id 17888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121055 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to write the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nUsing the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+4t \\\\ 2+5t \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf we solve for $t$ in the parametric equations of the line, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+4t \\\\ y=2+5t \\\\ z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly the first two equations have right-hand sides equal to $t,$ so we combine them but keep the third equation separate:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}, \\; z=3\n$$\n\nSo, it's as if we removed the $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ part in the original formula and replaced it with $z=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a2d263d-c94f-5d86-859a-c6c79c835eca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3316 :tutorial/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "c618a49e-3866-523c-b010-33f9b474436e" :step/math-academy-id 18457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120729}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119656 :question/id #uuid "74fb2af1-d791-5687-98cc-0b2db5bd8464" :question/math-academy-id "e-5021"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368681}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "669d1115-ca36-5052-810d-cb802269d1fa" :step/math-academy-id 18458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120710}}] :activity/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120015}]}
